# Findings that already exist. Measured **in CI**, on the Python and Node the workflow
# declares - not on a laptop: the first run reported 287 ruff and 1678 mypy where a
# local 3.14 interpreter measured 286 and 1675. The tool version is pinned in the
# workflow; the interpreter is pinned by the workflow too, and these are its numbers.
#
# 1678 is the CI's number, and it was lowered to 1675 by mistake: 1675 is what a local
# interpreter (3.14) measures, and the runner uses 3.11 - the same three-error gap that
# made this baseline 1678 in the first place. The ratchet caught it on the next run
# ("mypy grew from 1675 to 1678"), which is what a ratchet is for. Measure the baseline
# in CI, always: a laptop's number is not the number being enforced.
#
# ruff is pinned to 0.15.1 in the workflow, so its number is the same on a laptop and
# in CI (#274's 287 -> 286, and #231's 286 -> 284, both measured at that pin).
#
# These may only go DOWN. When a PR lowers one, lower it here in the same PR: that is
# how the debt is paid without blocking anyone on it (#235).
RUFF_BASELINE=284
MYPY_BASELINE=1678
TSC_BASELINE=13
